          "enhanced_information": "Carbon tetrachloride and diethylene glycol are known to be metabolized in the liver to toxic metabolites, leading to liver damage. Methanol is metabolized in the liver to formaldehyde and formic acid, which can also cause liver damage. Benzene, although metabolized in the liver to toxic intermediates, may contribute to liver damage but is not a primary toxic effect. Acetone, metabolized in the liver to acetone metabolites, is unlikely to cause significant liver damage. Therefore, the compounds most likely to produce liver damage as the main toxic effect are carbon tetrachloride and diethylene glycol."
